From 73bfa2fdacbc6b0c5e787211b90b37626063ba64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:09:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Bugfix: shift architecture-sized values, not ints, by
 hypercube dimensions [report R. Perry]

The number of vertices, the terminal domain bound, the domain size and
the bipartition bit of hypercube architectures were computed as
`1 << n`, which is undefined for n >= 31 whatever the size of `Anum`,
so that 64-bit builds, in which `archHcubArchLoad()` accepts dimensions
up to 62, produced garbage sizes. The same int shifts sized the
multinode arrays of sub-architecture and decomposition-defined
architecture matchings, the hash tables of fixed vertex handling, the
vertex count of distributed hypercube graphs, and the vertex and
terminal numbers of the `amk_ccc`, `amk_fft2`, `amk_hy`, `gmk_hy` and
`gmk_ub2` generators, which bounded their dimension from below only.

Shift a value of the target type everywhere. Move the dimension bounds
of `archHcubArchLoad()` into the new `archHcubArchBuild()` routine,
which `SCOTCH_archHcub()` now uses too, as it accepted any dimension.
Bound the dimension of each generator so that all of the values it
computes fit in a `SCOTCH_Num`.

Refs: COR-19, PS-API-3, API-10, CLI-24
